A||||14||Jaei Sun||My Battery in the front pix||||1||
Z||000000||Jaei Sun||04-10-2001||11:44 PM||||I thought I'd share with everyone how I moved my Battery to the front trunk.  I only had modify one thing, The drain hole in the front trunk was enlarged to allow for the 1 gauge positive to go thru.<P>Old Location:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/Batt_Remov_01.jpg"> <P>And Here's where it ended up:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/Batt_Front_01.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/Batt_Front_02.jpg"> <P>By doing it this way I didn't need a battery tray nor had to cut anything big.  The easy way to basically add ~80 lbs. to the front of the car.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||MikeSchaefer||04-10-2001||11:50 PM||||Looks great!  What do you ground it to?  Your way seems much more simple than the other ways I've seen of doing this.<BR>Later,<BR>Mike||||reg||6
Z||000002||Hym-The-Man||04-11-2001||12:18 AM||||What about flat repair?  How do you get the sare out?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hym-The-Man||04-11-2001||12:19 AM||||What about flat repair?  How do you get the spare out?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jaei Sun||04-11-2001||12:40 AM||||The Ground it attached to a thick part of the frame near the the jack stand "holder"... It's well grounded, no problems starting/with electrical.<P>If a flat ever occurs (I very well hope not- Z Rated tire$), I remove the sparetire brace/battery brace, lift the battery out and either set it on the front trunk lip by the radiator or on near the trunk latch.  That is why you see the extra positive & negitive cable (the neg it tucked off to the side).<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000005||Monkeyman||04-11-2001||04:51 AM||||Jaei Sun--For the record, Z rated tires have absolutely nothing to do with the possibility of getting a flat tire.  They aren't tougher tires.  Has more to do with how well they handle the heat generated by high speeds.  BTW--Tire ratings are highly, umm...overrated.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||04-11-2001||07:42 AM||||I think he's saying that he hopes he dosent get a flat in z-rated tires becuase they probably cost a lot.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||04-11-2001||11:05 AM||||Or, you can lift out the battery and use it as a big wheel chock!  Only negative side  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> to having to remove the battery in case of a flat, that I can foresee, is that you can't have your emergency flashers going.<P>Yeah, I know - 1)Put the battery back in after you remove the spare, or 2)Attach a portable flasher unit to the battery and set it by the car, or 3)Better yet, bring the battery over to the wheel you're working on, along with your 12-volt droplight!<P>Anyway, I like what I see.  It's simple, clean, looks reversible (I can try it and still go back if I don't like it).  Nice work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !               ||||reg||6
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||04-11-2001||11:09 AM||||80Lbs for a battery????????<P>Last I weighed mine it was closer to 40.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jaei Sun||04-11-2001||11:50 AM||||FieroJoe - Exactly... the Z's came with the rims and to my understanding are kind of pricey, so if one blew I would most likely buy another Z just to match the other three.<P>StuGood - The battery actually will stay wired,  You just lift it out and put it on the lip, with the cables still connected to it, so you can do the flat swap without ever disconnecting the battery.<P>Slammed - You're right the battery is 40 Lbs. Since the battery is required a component for the car to run, I'm relocating that 40 Lbs that was in the engine compartment to the front.  So not only am I adding 40 lbs. to the front of the car, but in the process I'm removing 40 lbs. from the back of the car -- effectively adding 80 lbs. to the front.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000010||DRH||04-11-2001||11:51 AM||||Probably meant -40 from the rear, +40 to the front has a "net" effect of 80 lbs?<P>I think the idea is the cables are long enough to set the battery aside, either on the cowl or the radiator, without disconnecting it.<P>What kind of battery is that? I wouldn't think mounting at a 45 degree angle would be a good idea with a conventional lead-acid battery.||||reg||1
Z||000011||lawrence||04-11-2001||12:56 PM||||I've never done this, so I have no reference.  But do you think it's a good idea to have the battery in that tilted position?  That will leave the top corners of the plate out of the acid.  At the very least, you will be reducing cranking current by the amount of surface area not wetted.<P>Is there roon to put a couple feet or a small wedge under the bottom to level it off?  Here's a link to Fieroman's page:<P> <A HREF="http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus/fieroman/fiero08.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus/fieroman/fiero08.jpg</A> <P>It looks like there's room.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A>  - WebElectric Magazine<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - Products<p>[This message has been edited by lawrence (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Jaei Sun||04-11-2001||01:13 PM||||Taken from Optima:<P>"... It is completely sealed and maintenance free, so it won't leak or corrode and is the safest and cleanest battery around. <B>The Optima can be mounted in any position - on its side or even upside down!</B> Resistant to heat and cold. Provides unequalled vibration resistance for all types of heavy duty, off-road and commercial applications. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000013||mshill||04-11-2001||03:43 PM||||One advantage I can see to having a box is that it lowers the 40 lbs you are putting up front down closer to the axle and will keep the center of gravity lower.||||reg||1
Z||000014||TheFiero64||04-11-2001||03:55 PM||||Cool Idea, I like it.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
